course: biology
lesson: 15
title: Monkeys: kinds, tails and troops
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: There are hundreds of kinds of monkeys, from a pygmy marmoset that could sit on your finger to a mandrill heavier than a big dog with a face painted red and blue.
explain: They come in two big families: New World monkeys of Central and South America with flat noses and sideways nostrils, and Old World monkeys of Africa and Asia with narrow noses and nostrils pointing down.
explain: Some New World monkeys have a fifth hand: a gripping tail with a bare, ridged tip like a fingerprint, so a spider monkey can hang by its tail alone and pick fruit with both hands.
explain: Most monkeys live in troops that groom each other, warn each other and shout at the neighbours: howlers boom at dawn, baboons march along the road, and snow monkeys sit together in hot springs.
explain: A gorilla is not a monkey at all: apes have no tail; and the cartoon monkey with a banana is wrong too, because wild monkeys never ate our sweet shop bananas.
fact: Spider monkeys have a prehensile, gripping tail with bare ridged skin under the tip; only some monkeys of Central and South America have gripping tails, and no monkey of Africa or Asia can hang by its tail.
fact: New World monkeys have flat noses with nostrils pointing sideways; Old World monkeys from Africa and Asia have narrow noses with nostrils pointing down and are closer relatives of apes and of us.
fact: Baboons live in big troops on the ground in Africa, sometimes 50 or more, with a ranking order, so many eyes spot lions and leopards and the troop defends each other; big males have teeth longer than a lion's, so never feed them or open car windows.
fact: Japanese macaques, or snow monkeys, at Jigokudani sit in hot springs in snowy winters to keep warm, and scientists found the baths lower their stress; they live farther north than any other monkey.
fact: Howler monkeys have a big hollow bone in the throat that makes their call heard about 5 kilometres away, so troops know where the others are without fighting.
fact: Bearded capuchins in Brazil crack hard palm nuts on a flat rock with a heavy stone, and the young take years to learn it by watching older monkeys.
fact: Mandrills of Central African rainforests are the biggest monkeys; a big male can weigh over 30 kilograms, with a red nose and blue ridges that are brightest on the strongest males.
fact: Male proboscis monkeys of Borneo have a long floppy nose that makes their calls louder; they swim well with partly webbed feet and jump into rivers from trees.
fact: Monkeys groom each other, picking out dirt, dead skin and bugs, which keeps fur clean, builds friendships and calms them down.
fact: Wild monkeys eat fruit, leaves, seeds, flowers and insects; our sweet bananas were bred by people, and Paignton Zoo in England stopped giving monkeys bananas because the sugar harmed their teeth and tummies.
fact: The pygmy marmoset from the Amazon is the smallest monkey, about 100 grams; it gnaws holes in bark to lap up gum and sap, and fathers and older brothers and sisters carry the babies, often twins.
fact: Gorillas, chimpanzees, bonobos and orangutans are apes, not monkeys: apes have no tail; gibbons, the acrobats of Asian forests, are smaller apes with no tail too.
exercise: What is special about a spider monkey's tail? | It grips like a fifth hand and has a bare ridged tip | It is short and fluffy like a rabbit's | It stores fat for the long dry season, like a camel's hump | It can be dropped off to escape a predator
exercise: Which monkeys can hang by their tails? | Only some monkeys of Central and South America | All monkeys in the world, big and small, in every forest | Only monkeys from Africa | Only monkeys from Asia
exercise: How can you tell a New World monkey by its nose? | A flat nose with nostrils pointing sideways | A long nose pointing down to its chin, like a beak | No nose at all, only two small holes in the face | A nose with one single nostril in the middle
exercise: Which monkeys are closer relatives of apes and of us? | Old World monkeys of Africa and Asia | New World monkeys of the Americas | Both are exactly as close as each other | Neither, monkeys are not related to apes or to people at all
exercise: Why do baboons live in big troops? | To spot lions and leopards and defend each other | Because they cannot find any food at all on their own | Because they are scared of the dark | Because each one needs a babysitter
exercise: Why do snow monkeys sit in hot springs? | To keep warm, and it lowers their stress | To wash off the snow before bed | To catch the fish that live in the warm water | To drink the hot water
exercise: Which monkeys live farther north than any other? | Japanese macaques, the snow monkeys | Baboons | Howler monkeys of the cold Andes mountains | Pygmy marmosets
exercise: How far can a howler monkey's call be heard? | About 5 kilometres | Only about 50 metres | About 500 kilometres | Only inside its own tree
exercise: Why do howler troops call at dawn? | To tell other troops where they are, so they need not fight | To wake up the sun | To call the rain down from the clouds so that the dry trees get water | To scare away mosquitoes
exercise: How does a bearded capuchin open a hard palm nut? | It smashes it on a flat rock with a heavy stone | It bites it with its strong back teeth | It drops it from the top of a very tall tree onto the rocks | It gives it to a parrot to crack
exercise: Which is the biggest monkey? | The mandrill | The pygmy marmoset | The mountain gorilla | The spider monkey
exercise: What does a male proboscis monkey's big nose do? | Makes his calls louder to attract females and warn rivals | Helps him breathe underwater when he swims | Lets him smell ripe fruit many kilometres away through the forest | Stores water for the dry season
exercise: Why do monkeys groom each other? | To clean the fur and build friendships | Because they are hungry for the bugs only | To pull out soft fur to line their nests | To count up how many hairs each other has
exercise: Why did Paignton Zoo stop giving its monkeys bananas? | Shop bananas are too sugary and harmed their teeth and tummies | The monkeys refused to eat them | Bananas became too expensive for the zoo to buy every week for all its monkeys | Bananas made the monkeys too fast
exercise: What do wild monkeys really eat? | Fruit, leaves, seeds, flowers and insects | Only bananas, picked from the wild banana trees in the forest | Only meat they hunt | Grass like a cow
exercise: How heavy is a pygmy marmoset? | About 100 grams | About 10 kilograms | Only about 1 gram | About 1 kilogram
exercise: Why is a gorilla not a monkey? | It is an ape, and apes have no tail | It is too black to be a monkey | It does not live in trees at all, only in caves | It is a kind of bear
test: Zoe at the zoo says look at that monkey, pointing at a gorilla. What should you tell her? | It is an ape, not a monkey: no tail | She is right, gorillas are the biggest monkeys | It is a kind of baboon | It is a monkey because it has hands
test: In a cartoon, a monkey from Africa hangs by its tail eating a banana. Name the two mistakes. | No African monkey can hang by its tail, and wild monkeys never ate sweet shop bananas | Monkeys never climb trees, and they never eat any kind of fruit at all | African monkeys have no tails and eat only meat | Nothing is wrong with that picture
test: A spider monkey hangs on with its tail and both feet and picks fruit with both hands. How many grippers does it have in all? | 5 | 4 | 2 | 6
test: 3 howler monkeys, 2 baboons. how many tails lol | 5 | 0 | 10 | 3
test: a baboon's at the car window at the safari park, should i give it my sandwich | No, keep the windows shut, they grab food and can bite | Yes, they are tame and gentle, and they love sandwiches | Yes, but only bread | Only if it says please
test: What would happen to a young capuchin that never watched the older monkeys crack nuts? | It would probably never learn to use the stones | It would know exactly how to do it from the day it was born | It would invent a better tool at once | It would crack the nuts with its teeth
test: Why could a pygmy marmoset live in a tree full of big monkeys without fighting them for food? | It eats gum and sap from holes it gnaws, food big monkeys cannot use | It eats only the biggest fruit first | It frightens the big monkeys away with its loud, high screams | It eats the big monkeys' leftovers only
test: Two baboons sit picking through each other's fur. My mate says they are just eating fleas. Is he right? | Only partly: grooming cleans the fur but mainly builds friendships and calms them | Yes, it is only a snack, and they stop as soon as the fleas are gone | No, they are fighting quietly | No, they are counting their babies
test: Which nose belongs to a monkey from Peru, in South America? | A flat nose with nostrils pointing sideways | A narrow nose with nostrils pointing down, like an African monkey's | A red nose with blue ridges | A long floppy drooping nose
test: A proboscis monkey falls out of a tree into a river. What happens next? | It swims away, it is a great swimmer with partly webbed feet | It sinks to the bottom, because no monkey can swim at all | It floats on its big nose | It waits for its troop to fish it out
test: A troop of 8 snow monkeys sits in the hot spring. How many ears are poking out? | 16 | 8 | 32 | 24
exam: Identify the principal distinction between monkeys and apes. | Apes possess no tail, whereas most monkeys possess one | Apes are invariably smaller than monkeys and possess longer tails | Monkeys possess hands, whereas apes possess hooves | Apes inhabit only the Americas
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the prehensile tail? | It is confined to certain monkeys of Central and South America | It is possessed by every species of monkey in Africa, Asia and the Americas | It is characteristic of the monkeys of Africa | It is characteristic of the great apes
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a zoological garden in England withdrew bananas from the diet of its monkeys. | The high sugar content of cultivated bananas impaired their teeth and digestion | The monkeys declined to consume them in any quantity, preferring the food of the visitors | The cost of procurement became excessive | Bananas were found to be poisonous to all primates
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the vocalisations of howler monkeys. | They may be heard at a distance of approximately five kilometres | They are inaudible beyond the tree occupied by the troop, being no louder than a whisper | They are produced exclusively at midnight | They are emitted solely for the purpose of attracting rain
